[SPEAKER_02]: You have to reflect on it frequently, for sure.

85
00:09:36,443 --> 00:09:37,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, give me some examples.

86
00:09:39,524 --> 00:09:40,524
[SPEAKER_02]: How would one reflect about it?

87
00:09:40,865 --> 00:09:47,067
[SPEAKER_02]: As the Buddha described those basic reflections that everybody, whether layman or gone forth, should cultivate.

88
00:09:48,108 --> 00:09:51,990
[SPEAKER_02]: That you're liable to things dear to you being taken away.

89
00:09:52,590 --> 00:09:56,252
[SPEAKER_02]: You're liable to things not dear to you, to being exposed to things not dear to you.

90
00:09:57,032 --> 00:09:58,533
[SPEAKER_02]: You're liable to whatever

91
00:09:59,190 --> 00:10:02,472
[SPEAKER_02]: Attachment you cultivate, you will have to experience the pain of that being taken away.

92
00:10:02,492 --> 00:10:09,256
[SPEAKER_01]: It sounds like all those reflections are... have that like a personal threat to them.

93
00:10:10,637 --> 00:10:19,542
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any other example you could give of such reflections that's not just phrases, some phrases from the suttas?

94
00:10:19,602 --> 00:10:20,743
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you have to make it concrete.

95
00:10:21,533 --> 00:10:22,894
[SPEAKER_02]: So, do you have friends?

96
00:10:23,174 --> 00:10:24,476
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you have family?

97
00:10:24,736 --> 00:10:25,456
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you have children?

98
00:10:26,197 --> 00:10:27,018
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you have a spouse?

99
00:10:27,498 --> 00:10:33,363
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you have possessions that, you know, make you wake up in the morning and feel enthusiastic about life?

100
00:10:34,805 --> 00:10:39,589
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have to reflect in regard to those being liable to destruction.

101
00:10:39,609 --> 00:10:42,872
[SPEAKER_02]: Not just abstractly, like, oh, yeah, I will lose what is dear to me.

102
00:10:42,912 --> 00:10:47,075
[SPEAKER_01]: But how will that reveal the danger of essentiality?

103
00:10:47,135 --> 00:10:47,436
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean...

104
00:10:48,285 --> 00:10:53,107
[SPEAKER_01]: I might reflect and say, I'm going to lose this in ten days, but until then I can enjoy it.

105
00:10:54,707 --> 00:11:00,289
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, but what's going to happen if you were cultivating the light for something and then you lose it?

106
00:11:00,329 --> 00:11:07,492
[SPEAKER_01]: But if I'm ready to lose it, say, in ten days, it's going away, I can't stop, it happened before, I'll be all right, but I'll enjoy it in the meantime.

107
00:11:07,632 --> 00:11:11,333
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you're ready to lose it, then why are you engaging in it now?

108
00:11:11,353 --> 00:11:13,333
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, because it provides pleasure, factual pleasure.

109
00:11:15,314 --> 00:11:16,234
[SPEAKER_01]: It's factually agreeable.

110
00:11:16,635 --> 00:11:17,495
[SPEAKER_01]: So why would I just

111
00:11:18,202 --> 00:11:19,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Waste nine days of enjoying it.

112
00:11:19,924 --> 00:11:20,604
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, that's the thing.

113
00:11:20,624 --> 00:11:30,733
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, if you... if you're honest with yourself, you'll see presence of the light means, automatically, liability to pain when that thing is taken away.

114
00:11:31,814 --> 00:11:34,596
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not a matter of then that the pain will come.

115
00:11:34,996 --> 00:11:39,700
[SPEAKER_02]: Right now, just by... on account of having the light, it's like hanging from a thread.

116
00:11:39,901 --> 00:11:41,582
[SPEAKER_02]: You're elevated hanging from a thread.

117
00:11:41,602 --> 00:11:45,065
[SPEAKER_02]: And when that thread breaks... Yeah, but until it breaks, I can enjoy it.

118
00:11:45,834 --> 00:11:46,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but if you came down...

119
00:11:46,695 --> 00:11:52,980
[SPEAKER_01]: If the danger... if the danger is when the thing breaks or changes, until then, I'll factually enjoy that happiness.

120
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,082
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, no, the danger is that you're hanging from a thread.

121
00:11:55,942 --> 00:12:11,255
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if instead of trying to come down from that thread, you keep enjoying what's up there, or keep enjoying the fruits at the top of the tree, as the Buddha said in that simile, then you can't be coming down at the same time, so that when the tree falls, you won't suffer.

122
00:12:12,957 --> 00:12:20,101
[SPEAKER_02]: So you, like, if you enjoy it, you're postponing saving yourself from the danger that's going to happen.

123
00:12:20,822 --> 00:12:38,613
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, in the example I gave, it's like in plenty of instances a person is willing or able and the mind is not necessarily even upset or bothered much by losing certain things, which kind of then

124
00:12:40,026 --> 00:12:41,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Almost like follows... the conclusion follows.

125
00:12:41,832 --> 00:12:44,361
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I can enjoy them until then, because I'm ready to give them up.

126
00:12:44,421 --> 00:12:45,746
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why wouldn't I enjoy them?

127
00:12:45,766 --> 00:12:46,849
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the danger of that?

128
00:12:51,242 --> 00:12:53,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, but... Because often that's kind of the point.

129
00:12:53,623 --> 00:13:03,385
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, okay, you know, the big attachments in this, I can see the danger of that, but, you know, I don't see any harm in tasting this, touching that, the kind of...

130
00:13:03,405 --> 00:13:04,645
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not attached to it.

131
00:13:04,665 --> 00:13:10,326
[SPEAKER_01]: And factually, like, it's simply not on that level of any attachment that you, like, oh, I'll lose sleep over this.

132
00:13:11,087 --> 00:13:12,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you still enjoy it.

133
00:13:13,467 --> 00:13:16,788
[SPEAKER_01]: And according to suttas, there's still danger in those things.

134
00:13:17,792 --> 00:13:19,114
[SPEAKER_01]: So, where's the mistake there?

135
00:13:19,134 --> 00:13:23,221
[SPEAKER_02]: So, we're still saying sensual things, not just... Yeah, yeah, sensual, sensual, of course, yeah.

136
00:13:23,802 --> 00:13:26,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Well... Like, sensuality in moderation.

137
00:13:26,327 --> 00:13:27,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Think of it like that.

138
00:13:27,148 --> 00:13:28,009
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's still sensual.

139
00:13:28,330 --> 00:13:28,851
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but...

140
00:13:29,890 --> 00:13:38,956
[SPEAKER_01]: You do... you kind of prevent yourself from getting too far with it, because that would be like one of the probably main things, practically speaking, for people.

141
00:13:38,996 --> 00:13:47,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, you might tell your environment to set things up rightly, but then there's going to be these things that are obviously not on that, like, a heavy level.

142
00:13:48,462 --> 00:13:53,846
[SPEAKER_01]: They are still sensual and it's like there's absolutely, well, nothing obviously dangerous about it.

143
00:13:55,427 --> 00:13:57,568
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what... what is that person missing?

144
00:13:58,289 --> 00:13:58,549
[SPEAKER_02]: Well,

145
00:13:59,716 --> 00:14:09,138
[SPEAKER_02]: If you allow delight and pleasure to the senses, you might be fine with those specific sense objects being taken away.

146
00:14:09,538 --> 00:14:16,080
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you ask yourself honestly, will I be fine if I lose my sight, if I go blind, if I go deaf?

147
00:14:16,920 --> 00:14:17,120
[SPEAKER_02]: Right.

148
00:14:17,841 --> 00:14:18,801
[SPEAKER_02]: Will I be fine with that?

149
00:14:18,941 --> 00:14:27,363
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you realize, well, all of these things, enjoying all these things, implies taking for granted, taking a safe,

150
00:14:28,155 --> 00:14:32,411
[SPEAKER_02]: That situation of being healthy, of being able to perceive these things.

151
00:14:34,012 --> 00:14:37,034
[SPEAKER_01]: So basically, okay, so that's the bigger picture they're missing out on.

152
00:14:37,415 --> 00:14:38,235
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the connection.

153
00:14:38,956 --> 00:14:59,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is like, you might factually be able to handle a lot of engagement with sensuality, because you don't have children, you don't have dependents, you don't have burden that would have become obvious through the years and so on, but you do carry the burden of, well, being intoxicated with your senses, with your health, taking it for granted.

154
00:15:00,153 --> 00:15:02,995
[SPEAKER_01]: So, every time you find joy in anything sensual,

155
00:15:04,416 --> 00:15:08,797
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the reflection needs to... like, basically, as we said, you need to make it personal.

156
00:15:09,177 --> 00:15:17,739
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how these impersonal sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches that I can factually handle, if I engage with, well, how do I make that personal as well?

157
00:15:18,659 --> 00:15:25,460
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't make sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches personal, because, again, they're irrelevant, and more often than not.

158
00:15:26,061 --> 00:15:30,822
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you can then make personal is, okay, so what... how would I engage with those objects?

159
00:15:30,982 --> 00:15:33,182
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the necessary basis for any engagement?

160
00:15:33,743 --> 00:15:40,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's the senses, it's the sights, it's the eyes, which means the implicit taking for granted of that basis is there.

161
00:15:41,207 --> 00:15:52,753
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, you start saying, oh, but if that were to change, if that were to go away, if that were to be lost or get sick, I would not be indifferent even towards these seemingly indifferent sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

162
00:15:53,313 --> 00:15:57,355
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, if you think you can handle sensuality,

163
00:15:59,664 --> 00:16:05,372
[SPEAKER_01]: To the extent you think you can handle it, to that extent you haven't taken it personally enough, the bigger picture.

164
00:16:07,234 --> 00:16:09,838
[SPEAKER_01]: So that would be another contemplation.

165
00:16:09,878 --> 00:16:15,045
[SPEAKER_01]: You could contemplate the obvious, as I said, attachments and dangers and things you fear, things you're anxious about.

